did you buy or sell crypto this year or  maybe use it to purchase something even  as small as a sandwich if so that  transaction might need to be counted on  your taxes  it's especially important to talk about  taxes during this 2022 tax filing season  because so many people  bought or sold crypto during 2021 for  the first time the number of crypto  owners worldwide nearly tripled in 2021  growing from 106 million in january to  295 million in december according to a  report by crypto.com for those crypto  newcomers wall street journal tax  columnist laura saunders has a warning  i think that the irs has a special focus  on crypto because they're uncomfortable  with it they see it as a kind of a  threat to the tax system they think it  may attract criminal activity money  laundering or tax evasion so the irs is  paying very special close attention to  cryptocurrency here are five things you  need to know about taxes for  cryptocurrencies  first how to report you'll see this  question on the form 1040 right below  your name be sure you answer it  accurately if you don't answer the  question or if you lie and they later  have a case against you involving crypto  it will probably be a lot easier for the  irs to win that case but once you check  the box how do you know how much you owe  to find out let's look at how the irs  defines the currency  since 2014 the internal revenue service  has classified cryptocurrencies as  property usually investment property  that means they're not like dollars or  euros they're much more like shares of  stock like apple or ibm shares and like  income on stocks and bonds the law has  different rules for short and long-term  gains on crypto  if you've held it for a year or less  it's a short-term capital gain or loss  if you've held it for longer than a year  it's a long-term capital gain or loss  and those gains if their long-term gains  can be taxed at much lower rates than  say wages so the top rate on wages might  be about 40 percent the top rate on the  long-term capital gain on cryptocurrency  would be 20  that's a big difference the good news is  that capital losses can be used to  offset the gains meaning you can pay  less in taxes for the money you gained  in crypto if you also lost money  investing in the currency  [Music]  but capital gains on an investment isn't  the only way people use crypto some are  using the currency to buy goods like  that sandwich or even a yacht if you're  holding your crypto in a taxable account  versus say a retirement account like an  ira and you use it to purchase something  then you have a taxable transaction this  isn't a new idea that's just as though  you were buying that item with shares of  stock say shares of apple stock and just  the way that if you  transferred the stock to someone you'd  have to tell the irs and have a loss or  gain the same thing is true with crypto  also similar to stocks the irs says  trading between two different entities  such as from apple stock to microsoft or  in crypto's case from bitcoin to  ethereum are taxable transfers  what we just discussed applies to buying  and selling cryptocurrency and other  tangible goods where it gets trickier is  if someone is paid for services in  crypto which happened on a big stage in  2021.  the mayors of both new york city and  miami tweeted their plans to accept at  least one paycheck in bitcoin plus more  businesses are offering pay in crypto to  attract younger workers  in that case it's compensation so you  owe income tax on it but you also owe  payroll taxes such as medicare taxes and  social security taxes after you pay  those taxes it becomes an investment but  if the crypto is a gift rather than a  payment for a service neither the giver  nor the recipient owes any income tax on  that transfer  but  when the  recipient ultimately sells the crypto  they have to use the giver's cost basis  to figure the capital gain or loss it's  not the value at the time the gift was  received it was the value when the giver  bought it and if that value goes up the  recipient would have to pay taxes on the  gain when they go to trade or sell so  keeping a clear record is key  many people have what they call hot and  cold wallets for security purposes but  this also helps organize records the  cold wall would be kind of like your  savings account you don't touch it the  hot wallet would be the one you're  trading from and so you could tell the  irs no don't you don't have to worry  about the cold wallet let's look at the  hot wallet this is the one i was working  with transferring crypto between these  two wallets is a non-taxable transaction  the irs's rules on cryptocurrency are  evolving as crypto itself evolves and  moves into the mainstream some digital  assets like nfts don't have official  guidance yet but many tax specialists  think they should be taxed like  collectibles such as art or antiques in  that case the top tax rate is 28 which  is higher than the top rate on  cryptocurrency trades  my advice would be  even if there aren't rules don't think  you can do whatever you want to do if  you don't report correctly the irs may  find out about it for instance  they've talked to judges and been  successful in getting customer records  from places like coinbase that they can  use to match against people's tax  returns and generate cases the tax  deadline for 2022 is april 18th for  further tax questions see the wall  street journal's 2022 tax guide  [Music]
 

